<p class="teaser-cap">Where a well-regularized Go1 policy's heat lives. The staircase everyone points at — the
  zero-order hold's own harmonics — is the sliver on the right: <b>1.6%</b> of the heat. The waste is one decade
  lower, in 5–25 Hz command content, and a causal predictive filter recovers about half of it.</p>

<section id="abstract">
  <h2>Abstract</h2>
  <div class="abstract">
  <p>Reinforcement learning (RL) locomotion policies frequently overheat commercial quadrupedal and humanoid
  actuators in operating regimes where vendor controllers remain thermally stable. The standard explanation
  attributes this dissipation to high-frequency command jitter, prescribing smoothness penalties in the reward
  function as the default remedy. However, this hypothesis has not been evaluated against the alternative
  explanation: that learned policies adopt postures demanding higher steady-state torque.</p>

  <p>We decouple these mechanisms by demonstrating that electrical copper loss decomposes into a posture term
  and a ripple term. Because these terms add in quadrature, their relative dominance depends strictly on joint
  steady-state torque. We locate this crossover threshold analytically and empirically at 3.2 N·m on a Unitree
  Go1 quadruped. Load-bearing calf joints operate above this threshold, carrying only 14–17% of their heat in
  bands accessible to filtering; conversely, hip abduction joints operate below it, carrying 33–45% of their
  thermal dissipation in the filter-reachable band.</p>

  <p>Evaluating post-hoc command filtering on frozen policies, our spectral decomposition reveals that
  zero-order hold (ZOH) staircase harmonics account for only 1.6% of total heat, whereas 5–25 Hz command
  content accounts for the remaining discretionary dissipation. Although open-loop evaluation suggests a 46%
  thermal reduction via zero-phase filtering, this ceiling collapses in closed loop. Introducing latency to
  eliminate phase lag degrades feedback stability, inducing falls in 20% of episodes at 1.0 m/s on
  well-regularized policies and in every episode on unregularized policies. Conversely, a causal predictive
  filter combined with joint-load gating safely recovers 47–54% of the reachable thermal budget across both
  regularized and unregularized checkpoints without inducing falls. Finally, we demonstrate that commonly
  deployed <code>action_rate</code> penalties fail to govern command-side thermal load, whereas effort
  penalties dictate the reachable budget.</p>
  </div>

  <ul class="contrib">
    <li><b>Spectral heat decomposition.</b> An exact Parseval-based split into steady posture, gait-synchronous
    torque, in-band jitter (5–25 Hz), and ZOH discretization harmonics (&gt; 25 Hz). Reachable budget: 18.1%
    for a well-regularized Go1 policy, 43.3% without effort penalties; ZOH harmonics only 1.6%.</li>
    <li><b>Thermal load crossover.</b> Out-of-band heating follows H = H₀ + κρ/(1−ρ) to within 0.45% residual.
    Posture overtakes ripple at τ̄ ≈ √Var(τ) ≈ 3.2 N·m. Joints below this threshold carry two to three times
    the filter-reachable heat share of joints above it.</li>
    <li><b>Closed-loop latency penalties.</b> The open-loop 46% thermal-reduction ceiling collapses to 8.5% in
    closed loop (Cliff's δ = −0.33). A 40 ms latency for zero-phase smoothing compromises feedback margins:
    20% fall rate at 1.0 m/s on regularized policies, 100% on unregularized ones.</li>
    <li><b>Predictive gated filtering.</b> An order-8 causal AR filter recovers 47% and 54% of the
    filter-reachable budget on regularized and unregularized policies respectively, with no falls.</li>
    <li><b>Reward ablation.</b> <code>action_rate</code> penalties raise step transients only 8% when ablated;
    effort and energy penalties govern 89% of the transient magnitude.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="kv">
    <div><b>18.1%</b><span>reachable budget</span><small>full-reward policy; 43.3% once effort terms are removed</small></div>
    <div><b class="warn">1.6%</b><span>ZOH staircase share</span><small>the defect the field points at carries almost none of the heat</small></div>
    <div><b>3.2 N·m</b><span>load crossover</span><small>below it, ripple dominates — 8 of Go1's 12 joints sit below</small></div>
    <div><b>8.5%</b><span>closed-loop recovery</span><small>against a 46% open-loop ceiling; Cliff's δ = −0.33, a <em>small</em> effect</small></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="s1">
  <h2>1. Introduction</h2>
  <p>Legged locomotion policies trained via reinforcement learning frequently encounter actuator thermal limits
  during real-world deployment. On a Unitree G1 humanoid running a whole-body controller, ankle-roll actuators
  reach approximately 90 °C within 5 to 10 minutes during static standing, whereas the manufacturer's
  controller maintains the identical joints between 30 °C and 45 °C<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref1">1</a></sup>.
  Similarly, on a Unitree A1 carrying a 3 kg payload, baseline RL policies trigger thermal protection in 5 to
  7 minutes<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref3">3</a></sup>.
  Conversely, on a Go2-W, a learned policy plateaus at 60 °C over 51 minutes of walking, whereas the vendor
  controller diverges to 84 °C and shuts down at 34 minutes<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref4">4</a></sup>. These
  observations confirm that policy behavior dictates thermal dissipation, yet the community remains divided
  regarding the governing mechanism.</p>

  <p>Two competing hypotheses currently prevail:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Posture Hypothesis.</strong> Thermal dissipation is governed by the steady-state holding
    torque demanded by the chosen kinematic posture. Supported by Go2-W trials, where a 24 °C difference at
    50 Hz was attributed to periodic foot lifting that relieved steady joint torque<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref4">4</a></sup>.</li>
    <li><strong>The Jitter Hypothesis.</strong> Excessive heating stems from high-frequency command
    oscillations that inflate RMS current without producing functional torque. Supported by the G1 ankle-roll
    failure, where static holding torque is fixed by gravity, yet controllers exhibit a 45–60 K temperature
    disparity<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref1">1</a></sup>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Neither hypothesis has measured both terms independently. Physical copper loss dictates that posture
  torque and dynamic torque ripple add in quadrature:</p>

  <div class="eq">
    <i>H</i> ∝ 𝔼[<i>τ</i><sup>2</sup>] =
    <span class="ub"><span><i>τ̄</i><sup>2</sup></span><em>posture</em></span> +
    <span class="ub"><span>Var(<i>τ</i>)</span><em>ripple</em></span>
  </div>

  <p>A zero-mean command ripple produces no net torque while contributing its full variance to thermal
  dissipation. Crucially, because the terms add in quadrature, a substantial steady torque <i>τ̄</i> suppresses
  the relative contribution of Var(<i>τ</i>). <strong>Whether command jitter governs thermal load is therefore
  a function of joint-specific loading rather than an intrinsic property of the policy.</strong></p>

  {{FIG1}}
</section>

<section id="s2">
  <h2>2. Actuation Pipeline and Problem Formulation</h2>
  <p>Commercial quadruped and humanoid architectures execute proportional-derivative (PD) loops directly on
  the motor drive rather than within the policy process. On Unitree systems, the policy executes at 50 Hz and
  commands are written at 500 Hz; DeepRobotics Lite3 deployments run policies at approximately 83 Hz over a
  1 kHz state loop. Actuator-level control frequencies reach 6 kHz for the Unitree GO-M8010-6 and 1 kHz for
  the DeepRobotics J60<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref6">6</a></sup>.</p>

  <p>A zero-order hold interfaces the policy to the low-level loop. At 50 Hz into 500 Hz, each command is held
  for 10 ticks before stepping discontinuously, producing an instantaneous proportional transient of
  <i>k<sub>p</sub></i> Δ<i>q</i> prior to rotor displacement.</p>

  <p>In standardized environments such as <code>unitree_rl_mjlab</code><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref29">29</a></sup>,
  PD gains are derived from a target closed-loop natural frequency of 10 Hz and a damping ratio of
  <i>ζ</i> = 2.0, with action scaling set to 0.25 <i>e</i>/<i>k<sub>p</sub></i>, where <i>e</i> is the effort
  limit. Consequently, an action reversal between successive policy steps commands a peak transient:</p>

  <div class="eq">
    <i>τ</i><sub>step</sub> = <i>k<sub>p</sub></i> · 2 ·
    <span class="frac"><span>0.25 <i>e</i></span><span><i>k<sub>p</sub></i></span></span>
    = 0.5 <i>e</i>
  </div>

  <p>A full-scale action reversal commands a transient equal to half the joint torque limit. With
  <i>ζ</i> = 2.0, the dominant closed-loop pole is at 2.68 Hz, whereas the 50 Hz command stream carries
  spectral content to the 25 Hz Nyquist limit.</p>
</section>

<section id="s3">
  <h2>3. Metrics</h2>

  <h3>3.1 Spectral heat decomposition</h3>
  <p>Applying Parseval's theorem to joint torque partitioned into mean and zero-mean components:</p>
  <div class="eq">
    𝔼[<i>τ</i><sup>2</sup>] = <i>τ̄</i><sup>2</sup> +
    <span class="big">∫</span><sub>0</sub><sup><i>f<sub>g</sub></i></sup> <i>S<sub>τ</sub></i>(<i>f</i>) d<i>f</i> +
    <span class="big">∫</span><sub><i>f<sub>g</sub></i></sub><sup><i>f</i><sub>Nyq</sub></sup> <i>S<sub>τ</sub></i>(<i>f</i>) d<i>f</i> +
    𝔼<sub><i>k</i></sub>[Var<sub>sub</sub>(<i>τ</i>)]
  </div>
  <p>with <i>f<sub>g</sub></i> = 5 Hz isolating gait frequencies and <i>f</i><sub>Nyq</sub> = 25 Hz the policy
  Nyquist. The last term collects intra-step variance above Nyquist — the ZOH's own harmonics. <strong>The sum
  of the final two terms defines the reachable share</strong> available to command-stream filtering.</p>

  <h3>3.2 Ripple fraction and out-of-band command power</h3>
  <div class="eq">
    <i>r</i> = <span class="frac"><span>Var(<i>τ</i>)</span><span><i>τ̄</i><sup>2</sup> + Var(<i>τ</i>)</span></span>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
    <i>ρ</i> = <span class="frac"><span><span class="big">∫</span><sub><i>f<sub>c</sub></i></sub><sup><i>f</i><sub>Nyq</sub></sup> <i>S<sub>q</sub></i>(<i>f</i>) d<i>f</i></span><span><span class="big">∫</span><sub>0</sub><sup><i>f</i><sub>Nyq</sub></sup> <i>S<sub>q</sub></i>(<i>f</i>) d<i>f</i></span></span>
  </div>
  <p><i>ρ</i> is computable from the command stream alone, with no robot, where <i>f<sub>c</sub></i> is the
  joint −3 dB closed-loop bandwidth.</p>
  <div class="note"><b>Flag 6 — symbol reused.</b> §3 defines <i>ρ</i> against the joint's −3 dB bandwidth
  (15.3 Hz), but Table 2's column header is <i>ρ</i><sub>&gt;5 Hz</sub>. Two different cutoffs under one
  symbol. Pick one and relabel. <sup class="cite flagref"><a href="#flag6">▲6</a></sup></div>

  <h3>3.3 Thermal proxy</h3>
  <p>We quantify motor thermal load via <i>H</i> = (1/<i>T</i>) ∫ <i>τ</i><sup>2</sup> d<i>t</i>, proportional
  to <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><sub>rms</sub><i>R</i> under a constant torque coefficient. Stator thermal dynamics
  justify the square rather than the absolute value: on calibrated brushless actuators the winding thermal
  time constant is 3.12 s against 777 s for the motor housing, a factor of 249<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref7">7</a></sup>.
  Driver-reported temperatures therefore lag winding heat badly<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref39">39</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref40">40</a></sup>,
  making current-based proxies the relevant primary signal. Effect sizes throughout are Cliff's
  δ<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref45">45</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section id="s4">
  <h2>4. Experimental Setup</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>E1 — single joint, synthetic commands.</strong> A second-order joint modeled on the Go1
    (<i>k<sub>p</sub></i> = 35, damping 0.5, armature 0.005 kg·m², <i>f<sub>n</sub></i> = 13.3 Hz,
    <i>ζ</i> = 0.60, −3 dB bandwidth 15.3 Hz) driven by a 2 Hz sinusoid with bandpass noise injected above
    bandwidth. Sampled at 50 Hz, held via ZOH to 500 Hz, integrated at 5 kHz. Load swept 0–20 N·m.</li>
    <li><strong>E2 — recorded commands, open loop.</strong> Trajectories from seven Go1 policies replayed
    through the single-joint model. Preserved motion is benchmarked against the ZOH trajectory low-passed at
    5 Hz, not the raw ZOH trajectory, which would penalise the filter for removing the jitter it exists to
    remove.</li>
    <li><strong>E3 — closed loop, full quadruped.</strong> A frozen Go1 policy in MuJoCo (50 Hz policy,
    500 Hz physics, 10 substeps). The filter operates inside the loop. Ten episodes of 500 steps across three
    commanded velocities under matched seeds and noise.</li>
    <li><strong>E5 — spectral decomposition.</strong> Baseline ZOH over 30 rollouts, three velocity commands.
    Spectral splits at 5 Hz and 25 Hz; intra-step variance supplies the &gt; 25 Hz component.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note"><b>Flag 4 — E4 does not exist.</b> The section promises four protocols and labels them
  E1, E2, E3, E5. The figure filenames (<code>e3-command-filters</code>, <code>e4-closed-loop</code>) follow a
  different numbering again. Renumber before submission.
  <sup class="cite flagref"><a href="#flag4">▲4</a></sup></div>

  <p><strong>Predictive filter.</strong> An order-8 linear autoregressive model<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref44">44</a></sup>
  fitted by least squares to the frozen policy's own ZOH rollouts, rolled forward two steps so a symmetric
  Hann kernel<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref43">43</a></sup> can be applied without waiting. No simulator
  interaction beyond calibration, no reward, no retraining.</p>
  <p><strong>Load gate.</strong> Joints whose measured mean |<i>τ</i>| on the ZOH baseline falls below the
  3.2 N·m crossover are filtered; the rest pass through untouched.</p>
</section>

