<section id="s6">
  <h2>6. Discussion and Practical Guidance</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Profile the spectral split before intervening.</strong> One baseline rollout establishes the
    reachable budget; halving it estimates attainable closed-loop recovery. If the reachable share is small,
    no command filter will save your motors and the effort belongs on the reward or the posture instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not eliminate phase lag via delay,</strong> and do not use aggressive cutoffs. Evaluate any
    filter at the top of the speed range, because that is the only place the failure appears.</li>
    <li><strong>Gate by joint load.</strong> The threshold is <i>τ̄</i> ≈ √Var(<i>τ</i>). Gating cost 2.5
    percentage points of heat and bought back most of the tracking.</li>
    <li><strong>Prioritize effort regularization over <code>action_rate</code></strong><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref57">57</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref58">58</a></sup>.
    If the goal is to protect motors, our evidence says penalise effort, not action difference.</li>
    <li><strong>Expect single-digit percentage gains</strong> on a loaded quadruped, and do not attribute them
    to jitter removal without checking the split.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="insight"><p><strong>Where this should pay much better.</strong> The favourable regime — very
  low steady torque — is not represented on a walking quadruped. It is exactly the condition of the reported
  G1 failure: an ankle-roll joint on a standing humanoid. That is where the hardware experiment should look
  first, and it is a prediction this work makes rather than a result it demonstrates.</p></div>
  <p>The 80% power reduction reported for smoothness regularization on a quadrotor<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref5">5</a></sup>
  does not transfer, and we can say why: a rotor has almost no steady holding torque, so its
  <i>τ̄</i><sup>2</sup> term is small, its gait-band term does not exist, and the ripple term dominates by
  default. A stance-phase knee is the opposite case in every one of those three respects.</p>
</section>

<section id="s7">
  <h2>7. Related Work</h2>
  <p><strong>Thermal-aware locomotion.</strong> Recent methods add motor temperature to the observation and
  thermal constraints to the reward<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref2">2</a></sup>, or move the correction into
  a residual policy conditioned on thermal state<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref3">3</a></sup>. Thermal
  management by posture was earlier posed as a contact-constrained inverse-kinematics problem on a
  humanoid<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref9">9</a></sup>. Model-free prediction of joint-motor thermal
  states<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref52">52</a></sup> and lumped-parameter thermal
  networks<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref41">41</a></sup> supply the modelling layer this work proxies rather
  than models. All retrain or instrument; our intervention does neither.</p>

  <p><strong>Actuation interfaces.</strong> Actuator Reality Shaping equips each joint with a
  two-degree-of-freedom controller shaping the physical actuator to match simulation's reference
  dynamics<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref10">10</a></sup>; high-frequency linear feedback at up to 40 kHz
  interpolates learned torque policies<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref11">11</a></sup>; commanded accelerations
  have been constrained to a voltage-realizable set<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref12">12</a></sup>. Actuator
  networks learned from bench data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref22">22</a></sup> and domain
  randomization<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref34">34</a></sup> or online system
  identification<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref35">35</a></sup> are the standard alternatives. Post-optimization
  of chunked action sequences exists for manipulation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref13">13</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref36">36</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref37">37</a></sup>,
  as does a B-spline action representation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref14">14</a></sup> and real-time
  chunking, which handles inference latency by inpainting rather than by
  delaying<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref38">38</a></sup> — the closest prior art to our predictive filter.
  On cost-constrained quadrupedal hardware a measured transport delay above 50 ms is handled with a forward
  model and a time-aware network<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref15">15</a></sup>. None of these targets
  electrical loss.</p>

  <p><strong>Smoothness regularization.</strong> CAPS<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref5">5</a></sup>,
  Lipschitz-constrained policies<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref16">16</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref48">48</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref54">54</a></sup>
  and spectral normalisation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref17">17</a></sup> all reduce high-frequency content;
  a benchmark study reports its best hybrid method improving control smoothness by 26.8% for a worst-case
  2.8% performance degradation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref18">18</a></sup>, and the regularizer design
  space continues to be re-examined<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref49">49</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref50">50</a></sup>.
  None reports motor current or temperature on a legged robot.</p>

  <p><strong>Control rate and discretization.</strong> Robust locomotion has been demonstrated at 8 Hz on
  ANYmal C<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref19">19</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref32">32</a></sup>,
  with low-rate policies shown to be <em>less</em> sensitive to actuation latency; Q-learning degenerates as
  the time step shrinks<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref20">20</a></sup>; delayed MDPs admit augmented-state
  solutions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref46">46</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref47">47</a></sup>; and
  policies that choose their own action durations reduce average control frequency without losing
  reward<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref21">21</a></sup>. To our knowledge no paper reports motor temperature
  or current as a function of policy control rate.</p>

  <p><strong>Platforms and stacks.</strong> The quasi-direct-drive actuation that makes copper loss the
  dominant term is established in<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref30">30</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref31">31</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref56">56</a></sup>,
  with series-elastic ANYdrive as the contrasting design<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref32">32</a></sup>.
  Policies of the kind studied here are trained in <code>legged_gym</code><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref25">25</a></sup>
  on Isaac Gym<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref26">26</a></sup>, Isaac Lab<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref27">27</a></sup>,
  MuJoCo Playground<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref28">28</a></sup> or mjlab<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref29">29</a></sup>,
  deployed with stacks such as<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref33">33</a></sup>, and derive from the
  teacher-student line of<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref22">22</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref23">23</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref24">24</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section id="s8">
  <h2>8. Limitations</h2>
  <p><strong>No hardware.</strong> Everything here is simulation. 𝔼[τ²] is proportional to copper loss only
  under a constant torque constant; it ignores iron loss, inverter loss and every thermal path. The claims
  are about a <em>proxy</em>.</p>
  <p><strong>The single-joint model has no contact.</strong> E1 and E2 use a second-order joint with a
  constant load. Real stance-phase torque is neither constant nor independent of the command.</p>
  <p><strong>One robot, one gait family, one policy family,</strong> all from the same training setup on flat
  terrain.</p>
  <p><strong>The 5 Hz band split is a modelling choice, and the headline number depends on it.</strong> A
  lower boundary would make the reachable share larger and a higher one smaller. The 25 Hz boundary, by
  contrast, is not a choice: it is the command Nyquist. A sweep of the 5 Hz line would make the budget claim
  considerably more solid, and we have not done it.</p>
  <p><strong>"Reachable" is an upper bound, not an achievable target.</strong> Some 5–25 Hz torque content is
  the robot's genuine response to contact, not command jitter, and removing it costs stability. The recovery
  ratio of about a half may be exactly this: the fraction of the band that is truly discretionary.</p>
  <p><strong>The reward-ablation evidence is single-seed,</strong> and success is measured as tracking, not
  as task completion over long horizons.</p>
  <p><strong>Depth of reading of the cited literature.</strong> Existence, venue and authorship of every
  reference below were verified by search during preparation. That is a weaker claim than having read each in
  full, and §7's characterisations should be read accordingly.</p>
</section>

<section id="s9">
  <h2>9. Conclusion</h2>
  <p class="lead">Actuator overheating in learned legged locomotion decomposes into steady posture torque and
  dynamic command ripple. Because these components combine in quadrature, command filtering provides
  meaningful thermal relief only on lightly loaded joints operating below the load crossover threshold. In
  closed loop, phase lag introduced by smoothing compromises feedback stability at speed, demonstrating that
  causal predictive filtering with load-dependent gating is required to safely recover actuator thermal
  margins.</p>
</section>

